# ===========================================================================
# {{PROJECT_NAME}} — what is never committed.
#
# THE ANCHORING RULE, which the compliance test enforces:
# a bare `figures/` matches EVERY directory called figures at any depth, including
# `output/figures/`. When a name should only match at the repository root, it carries a
# leading slash. Every rule below that names one of the tree's directories is anchored;
# the unanchored rules are the ones that genuinely must match anywhere (`__pycache__/`).
#
# THE `**` + directory re-inclusion IDIOM, used for data/ and output/:
#     /output/**            ignore everything under output/, at any depth
#     !/output/**/          but still DESCEND into its directories
#     !/output/**/.gitkeep  and keep the structure markers
# The middle line is load-bearing. Without it git never enters `output/figures/`, so the
# `.gitkeep` negation below can never match and a fresh clone has no output tree.
# ===========================================================================

# --- Raw and processed data ------------------------------------------------
# Raw data is irreplaceable and usually not ours to redistribute; processed data is a
# cache that any preprocessing script rebuilds. Neither belongs in git history.
/data/**
!/data/**/
!/data/**/.gitkeep

# --- Everything the code generates ----------------------------------------
# One root for generated files means one rule here. The exceptions below are the small
# text artefacts that ARE the record of a run and are worth having in git.
/output/**
!/output/**/
!/output/**/.gitkeep
!/output/All_Results.md
!/output/figures/CAPTIONS.md
# PNGs are committed on purpose — they are the low-DPI renders, small enough to review in
# a diff and to look at on GitHub without cloning.
!/output/figures/**/*.png
# PDFs are the print-quality twins of those PNGs: regenerable, vector, and large. The
# paper pulls them from a local run, not from git.
/output/figures/**/*.pdf

# --- Model weights --------------------------------------------------------
# Hundreds of MB each, and either downloaded from upstream or reproducible from a run.
/checkpoints/**
!/checkpoints/**/
!/checkpoints/**/.gitkeep
*.ckpt
*.pt
*.pth
*.safetensors
*.npz

# --- Environments and tool caches -----------------------------------------
# Unanchored on purpose: these appear at any depth and are never wanted anywhere.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
env/
*.egg-info/
.eggs/
build/
dist/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# --- Editor and OS --------------------------------------------------------
# `.vscode/` is ignored EXCEPT two files that are shared on purpose. WHY: without
# `python.analysis.extraPaths`, Pylance cannot resolve `from src.utils...` in a fresh clone
# (the repository uses a flat `src/` package root, not `src/<package>/`), so every import in
# every file is underlined red until someone runs the editable install. That is not a
# personal preference, it is the project's layout, so it belongs in git.
/.vscode/*
!/.vscode/settings.json
!/.vscode/extensions.json
.idea/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# --- Local secrets and overrides ------------------------------------------
# Anchored: a project-level .env only, never a fixture called .env inside tests/.
/.env
/.env.local
